{"name":"Castaway Cove","hex":"#7a9291","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"castaway","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cast adrift or ashore; marooned.","Shipwrecked."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shipwrecked sailor.","A discarded person or thing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/castaway"},{"term":"cove","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hollow in a rock; a cave or cavern. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A concave vault or archway, especially the arch of a ceiling."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To arch over; to build in a hollow concave form; to make in the form of a cove."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cove"}],"entries":[{"title":"Castaway","blocks":[{"text":"A castaway is a person who is cast adrift or ashore. While the situation usually happens after a shipwreck, some people voluntarily stay behind on a desert island, either to evade captors or the world in general. A person may also be left ashore as punishment (marooned)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e2/Rescue_by_seamen_of_USNS_Passumpsic.jpg/330px-Rescue_by_seamen_of_USNS_Passumpsic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castaway","wikidata":"Q508230"},{"title":"Cove","blocks":[{"text":"A cove is a small bay or coastal inlet. They usually have narrow, restricted entrances, are often circular or oval, and are often situated within a larger bay. Small, narrow, sheltered bays, inlets, tidal creeks, or recesses in a coast are often considered coves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/McWay_cove_1.jpg/330px-McWay_cove_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cove","wikidata":"Q31615"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Desertisland.jpg/500px-Desertisland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Desertisland.jpg/1280px-Desertisland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Desertisland.jpg/500px-Desertisland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Castaways may need to survive on a desert island.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADesertisland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg/330px-Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg/330px-Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Robinson Crusoe (1719) by Daniel Defoe. Illustration of Crusoe standing over Man Friday after freeing him from the cannibals.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a8/Figure_lulworth_formation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a8/Figure_lulworth_formation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Map showing two examples of how coves form. The rock types are those of Lulworth Cove. In example A, a river breaks through the resistant chalk back rock and limestone, leaving the weak clays to be rapidly eroded. In example B, the sea breaks through the limestone, perhaps by forming a cave, and then erodes the clay away.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFigure_lulworth_formation.pn